1.1.text:
Article 23

1. Implementation of Protection for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in Article 13 letter j in order to provide legal protection and / or health recovery.
2. Protection for Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:a. legal aid;
b. assistance following the occurrence of acts of violence;
c. rehabilitation and social reintegration ; dand. health services .

Article 13

Implementation of protection of women is provided to:

a. female workers/laborers;
b. elderly women;
c. women with disabilities;
d. homeless women;
e. domestic workers;
f. female heads of household;
g. female former inmates of correctional institutions;
h. female disaster victims;
i. female commercial sex workers; and
j. female victims of violence.

Article 17

1. Implementation of protection for homeless women as referred to in Article 13 letter d is in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection rights for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

CHAPTER XII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 28

1. The Regent or SKPD responsible for women's empowerment conducts guidance and supervision over women's empowerment and protection.
2. The form of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is as follows:a. providing outreach/dissemination to the community and business sector regarding women's empowerment and protection policies;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding women's empowerment and protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c. facilitating capacity building of institutions implementing women's empowerment and protection;
d. facilitating forums for women's participation and women's organizations;
e. facilitating rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disasters and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and protection; dang. providing awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally that have made efforts in women's empowerment and protection.
3. The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities over women's empowerment and protection.

CHAPTER II

SCOPE

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. principles and objectives;
b. women's rights;
c. obligations and responsibilities of regional government;
d. women's empowerment;
e. women's protection;
f. implementation of women's protection;
g. strategy for women's empowerment and protection;
h. mechanism for organizing women's empowerment and protection;
i. financing;
j. guidance and supervision;
k. transitional provisions; and
l. closing provisions.

Article 14

1. Implementation of protection for female workers/laborers as referred to in Article 13 letter a is to provide protection from acts of economic exploitation and cruel, inhumane treatment and to seek proper appreciation for work.
2. Female workers/laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. health and social security;
c. access to information and legal consultation services;
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status; and
e. witnesses and victims.

CHAPTER IV

WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 5

1. Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage.
2. Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to their married life, relations with their children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
3. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

